from lib import runs
from content_a import note, figcap
import resnums as N
from resnums import f, sg, ref, P1

EQ6 = ('<m:r><m:t>\u0394</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t>=</m:t></m:r><m:f><m:num><m:r><m:t>1</m:t></m:r></m:num><m:den><m:r><m:t>2</m:t></m:r></m:den></m:f>'
       '<m:d><m:e><m:sSub><m:e><m:r><m:t>\u03c1</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>within,NV</m:t></m:r></m:sub></m:sSub><m:r><m:t>+</m:t></m:r>'
       '<m:sSub><m:e><m:r><m:t>\u03c1</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>within,VPN</m:t></m:r></m:sub></m:sSub></m:e></m:d><m:r><m:t>\u2212</m:t></m:r>'
       '<m:sSub><m:e><m:r><m:t>\u03c1</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>cross</m:t></m:r></m:sub></m:sSub>')

def calib(D):
    fpc = {c: N.flows_per_capture(c) for c in ("novpn", "vpn")}
    import numpy as np
    med = {c: float(np.median(list(v.values()))) for c, v in fpc.items()}
    D.p(f"*Calibrating the grouping.* The processed files do not record which capture a flow came from, but the released captures can be counted. The public list of capture-file prefixes in the Deep Packet code [7] names {sum(N.CAPTURE_FILES['novpn'].values())} Non-VPN and {sum(N.CAPTURE_FILES['vpn'].values())} VPN capture files for CHAT, FT, MAIL, STREAMING and VOIP, plus one VPN P2P capture; BROWSING has no dedicated captures. Dividing each class's flows in the 15 s files by its number of captures gives {min(fpc['novpn'].values()):.0f}\u2013{max(fpc['novpn'].values()):.0f} flows per capture without a VPN (median {med['novpn']:.0f}) and {min(fpc['vpn'].values()):.0f}\u2013{max(fpc['vpn'].values()):.0f} with one (median {med['vpn']:.0f}), and about 80% of that in a training partition. Non-VPN captures are therefore closest to the setting of about 35 flows per cluster, and VPN captures to the coarsest setting or beyond. We added a calibrated design in which the number of clusters for each class equals its number of capture files (at least four), and BROWSING gets as many clusters as its flows divided by the context's median flows per capture. The estimate is rough: the prefix list comes from third-party code, P2P has at most one capture per context, a capture is not necessarily one session, and a 15 s timeout splits a long session into many flows.")

def methods(D, O):
    D.h1("IV. METHODOLOGY")
    D.h2("A. Overview")
    D.p("Fig. 2 sketches the analysis. Primary models and rankings are fitted on the primary partitions. Three within-context references describe how much rankings move without any change of context (Section IV-D). On top of that we test class composition directly, retrain without the minimum-IAT features and compare with permutation importance, aggregate importance into feature families, measure two practical consequences of explanation differences, and run sentinel and robustness checks.")
    n = D.figure(N.RES.parent / "figures" / "fig2_pipeline.png", "Experimental pipeline. Each context is modelled on its own. The repeated designs redraw the 80/20 partition in every repeat, and the consequence tests select features inside training partitions only.")
    D.h2("B. TreeSHAP and Global Importance")
    for i in (63, 64, 65, 66): D.add(O[i])
    D.p("Averaging over output classes leaves one global ranking for each model and context. That is not the same as balancing the true classes among the explained records. Each test record still counts once, so frequent classes contribute more records; we therefore also report importance with true classes weighted equally. Neither version speaks to class-specific or instance-level consistency.")
    D.add(O[68])
    D.h2("C. Ranking Agreement Measures")
    for i in (70, 71, 72, 73, 74, 75, 76): D.add(O[i])
    D.p("Spearman and Kendall weight a swap between ranks 20 and 21 as heavily as one between ranks 1 and 2, and the bottom third of each list carries only about 0.2–3% of importance per feature (Fig. 4). We therefore add two measures that emphasise the top. Rank-biased overlap [45] averages the overlap of the two Top-d sets with depth weights (1 − p)p^{d−1} that shrink by a factor p at each further depth d; we use the extrapolated form with p = 0.9. These depth weights sum to 1 − 0.9^{10} ≈ 0.65 over the first ten depths, but the agreement at rank i enters the overlap at every depth d ≥ i, so the first ten ranks carry about 86% of the total weight (Webber et al. [45], Eq. 21). The second is the L1 distance between the normalised importance vectors, the sum over features of |s_{j} − s′_{j}|, which is twice the total-variation distance and ranges from 0 to 2. It compares the shares themselves, so features with negligible shares barely contribute.")
    D.p("In short, we report Spearman, Kendall, Top-k overlap together with its chance-corrected form κ_{k} from (5), RBO and the L1 share distance. With m = 23, κ_{5} = 0.49 corresponds to three shared features out of five and κ_{10} = 0.65 to eight out of ten. Whole-ranking agreement and agreement at the top are read together, because either one alone can make two explanations look more interchangeable than they are.")
    D.h2("D. Within-Context References and Permutation Null")
    D.p("A cross-context comparison needs a reference: how far do rankings move when the context does not change? We use three designs, the grouped one with two kinds of groups (feature clusters and blocks of consecutive rows) at several settings, each repeated 11 times for both models. In every repeat the deduplicated flows of each context are split afresh into stratified 80% training and 20% test partitions, with a seed that changes from repeat to repeat, so the spread over repeats includes partition-level variation as well as training and seed variation.")
    D.p("*Flow-level random half-splits.* The training partition of each context is split, stratified by class, into two disjoint halves, and each half is subsampled (again stratified) to at most 3,477 records (3,476–3,477) so that all halves have the same size in both contexts. Each half-model gets its own random_state and is explained on the test partition of its repeat. Within-context agreement compares the two halves of one context; cross-context agreement averages the four Non-VPN-half versus VPN-half pairs of the same repeat. The gap is")
    D.eq_omml(EQ6, 6)
    D.p("computed per repeat, and in the same way for RBO, Top-5 overlap, κ_{5} and family-level ρ.", indent=False)
    D.p("*Cluster-grouped half-splits.* Random halves put near-duplicate flows from one session on both sides, which can inflate within-context agreement. As a proxy for sessions, the training flows of each class are clustered with k-means on log(x + 2)-standardised features. The number of clusters is set so that the average cluster holds about 10, 35 or 140 flows, with at least four clusters per class. Whole clusters are assigned at random to the two halves, larger clusters first, each to the currently smaller half. Each class is then subsampled to the size of its smaller half, and the halves of both contexts are subsampled to a common size (3,368–3,476 records). Only the division of the training partition into halves is grouped: the outer 80/20 partition remains a random stratified split, so the explained test records are not separated by cluster from either half, and the design tests sensitivity to grouping, not session independence. About 10 flows per cluster keeps only very close neighbours together. About 140 also separates broader regions of feature space, which is why the half-models' macro F1 falls as the clusters grow (Table VII).")
    calib(D)
    w = N.LOC[N.LOC.lag == "win20"].ratio
    D.p(f"*Row-order blocks.* The clusters are built from the same features that the models use, so at coarse settings they may separate the halves by region of feature space rather than by capture. A second proxy does not use the features. In the released Scenario A2 ARFF files, flows are sorted by class and, within a class, appear in the order in which the flow meter wrote them. Every deduplicated flow matches exactly one ARFF row, so its position in the file is known. Neighbouring rows are much more alike than random rows of the same class: in every class and context, the nearest of the 40 rows within 20 positions lies at {f(w.min(),2)}\u2013{f(w.max(),2)} times the distance of the nearest of 40 random rows, and the similarity fades over tens to a few hundred rows. This suggests that the files keep flows from the same capture close together, although their documentation does not say so. We form halves from contiguous blocks of rows within each class: blocks of about 10, 35 or 140 training flows, as many blocks as the class has capture files (calibrated, as above), and, as the strictest setting, the first versus the second half of each class. Blocks are assigned to halves in the same way as clusters. This grouping has its own weaknesses. Equal-sized blocks do not follow capture boundaries, so a capture can straddle two blocks and reach both halves, and the first-versus-second-half split may also separate different applications within a class.")
    D.p("*Permutation null.* For each class, the flows of both contexts are pooled and randomly reassigned to two pseudo-contexts that keep the original per-class counts. The pseudo-contexts therefore inherit the class mixes of Non-VPN and VPN but not their capture conditions. The flow-level random design is then applied to the pseudo-contexts and Δ is computed in the same way. Under this null, Δ shows what the half-split procedure and the class-mix difference produce when nothing else separates the two contexts.")
    rf800 = min(P1["rf800_vs_full_novpn"] + P1["rf800_vs_full_vpn"])
    D.p(f"XGBoost half-models are explained on the full test partition of each repeat. Random Forest TreeSHAP is much slower, so each Random Forest half-model is explained on a class-stratified sample of 800 test records; rankings from such samples agree with the full-test ranking at ρ ≥ {f(rf800)} for the primary models and at ρ ≥ {f(min(x['rho'] for x in N.P5['rf800_halfmodels']))} for eight half-models refitted and explained on their full test partitions, with the same five leading features in every case. Importance is also computed with true classes weighted equally. The repeats share one dataset and overlap in their test records, so they are not independent replications. We use the standard deviation and range of Δ across repeats to describe its spread, not as a standard error. The other ISCX time windows (30, 60 and 120 s) are recomputed from the same captures and cannot serve as an independent second pair of contexts.")
    D.h2("E. Class-Composition Tests")
    D.p("Three checks address class mix. First, global importance is recomputed with true classes weighted equally, on the primary fits and in every half-split repeat. Second, in a class-mix swap, each context's training partition is resampled without replacement to 3,400 flows with the other context's class proportions, the largest size both contexts allow. As a control, it is also resampled to 3,400 flows with its own proportions. Both models are explained on the context's own test partition and compared with the primary rankings of both contexts, using five draws for XGBoost and three for Random Forest. If class mix drove the cross-context disagreement, the swapped models should move toward the other context's ranking. Third, class-matched training uses 190 training flows per class, the number of Non-VPN MAIL flows. Six draws of 1,330 flows per context are compared with a same-size within-context reference built from two disjoint draws with each context's natural class mix.")
    D.h2("F. Aggregated Feature-Family Importance")
    D.p("Table I sorts the 23 features into six semantic families: one flow-duration feature, eight directional IAT statistics, two throughput features, and four statistics each for flow IAT, active periods and idle periods. For a family G, we add up the global importances I_{j} of its members from (2) and then normalise across the six families. Because absolute values are taken before summing, member contributions of opposite sign cannot cancel. The result is aggregated feature-family importance; it is neither instance-level Group SHAP nor a Shapley game with families as players.")
    corr = P1["corr_novpn"], P1["corr_vpn"]
    lo = min(corr[0]["duration|total_fiat"], corr[0]["duration|total_biat"], corr[1]["duration|total_fiat"], corr[1]["duration|total_biat"])
    hi = max(corr[0]["duration|total_fiat"], corr[0]["duration|total_biat"], corr[1]["duration|total_fiat"], corr[1]["duration|total_biat"])
    D.p(f"Two features of this grouping affect what it can show. Families have different sizes, so a large family can lead a ranking by size alone; we therefore also rank families by the mean importance of their members. And the grouping is a choice: total_fiat and total_biat correlate with duration at Spearman {f(lo,2)}–{f(hi,2)} yet sit in the directional family, while duration forms a family of one. We therefore also use an alternative grouping that moves the two totals into a duration-and-totals family, and data-driven clusters formed by average-linkage clustering of 1 − |ρ_{{S}}|, where ρ_{{S}} is the Spearman correlation between two features averaged over the two training partitions, cut at 0.2. Family-level agreement is computed on the primary fits and in every half-split repeat.")
    D.h2("G. Consequence Tests")
    D.p("A difference between explanations has practical weight if acting on it changes predictive performance. We test two actions. In *direct model transfer*, the primary model of one context is applied unchanged to the test partition of the other. This shows whether the learned mapping from features to applications carries over at all, and it is the scenario that motivates the study: a classifier trained without a VPN and deployed where traffic is tunnelled, or the reverse.")
    D.p("In *feature-list transfer*, selection uses training data only [52–54]. Each training partition is split into a stratified 80% inner-training and 20% inner-validation subset. A selector is trained on the former, its TreeSHAP importance is measured on the latter (for Random Forest, on a stratified sample of 800 inner-validation records), and the k highest-ranked features are retained. A reduced model is then refitted on the whole training partition with those features and scored on the untouched test partition. The own list comes from the target context, and the transferred list from the other context with the same inner-split seed. We run k = 5, 10 and 15 for both models, give them equal weight, and repeat the selection with five inner-split seeds. Because each list is scored on both targets, the four scores of a model form a 2 \u00d7 2 design of list origin by target, which separates a context-match effect from a difference in overall list quality.")
    D.p("Two controls show whether SHAP-based selection matters at all: the ten lowest-ranked features of the target-context selector (bottom-10), and 20 random 10-feature subsets that are drawn once and used for every model and target. For the first selection seed at k = 10, 3,000 class-stratified paired bootstrap draws of the test records give percentile intervals for the own-minus-transferred difference. These intervals hold the fitted models fixed and leave out training and selection uncertainty, which the five seeds show separately.")
    D.h2("H. Sentinel Representations and Robustness Configurations")
    D.p("In the processed features, −1 stands for an undefined statistic. The sentinel experiment builds ten indicator variables, one each for min_fiat, min_biat, max_fiat, max_biat, min_flowiat, max_flowiat, min_active, max_active, min_idle and max_idle, equal to one when the source feature is −1. The primary representation keeps the raw sentinels in all 23 features. In S1, −1 stays in the original features and the ten indicators are appended; in S2, −1 is replaced by NaN, which both tree models handle natively, and the same indicators are appended. Across both partitions, min_fiat is −1 in 12.6% of Non-VPN and 10.0% of VPN flows, min_biat in 16.1% and 16.6%, the active and idle minima and maxima in 65.0% and 53.5%, and min_flowiat in only 0.3% and 0.4%. Ranks are compared over the 23 original features, and the indicators' share is reported separately.")
    D.p("The robustness analysis crosses three tree models (Random Forest, XGBoost and Extra Trees [50]) with the path-dependent and interventional TreeSHAP formulations. Each model is explained on a class-stratified sample of 500 test records of its own context (seed 42). For interventional TreeSHAP, the background is a separate class-stratified set of 200 training records from the same context, all of which are used. Percentile 95% intervals for cross-context ρ come from 2,000 resamples of the explained records within each context, with models and backgrounds fixed; they reflect explained-record noise only. We also refit XGBoost with three other seeds and with max_depth = 3 and 10.")
    D.h2("I. Statistical Reporting")
    D.p("We report effect sizes and their spread, not significance tests. For consistency, the effect is Δ in Spearman units (and the analogous gaps in RBO, Top-5 overlap and κ_{5}), and its spread is the standard deviation and range over the 11 repeats of each design. Because these repeats share one dataset and overlapping test records, ratios of Δ to its standard deviation are not test statistics, and we do not report them. For the consequence tests, effects are paired differences in macro F1, read against the spread over selection seeds and against the random-subset controls. All intervals are marginal 95% percentile intervals, and none allows for clustering by capture or session.")
